1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:12,950
This is River Harpoon: a simple spear-fishing game for low-vision research, running here on a PC.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,450
Aim with the mouse, the arrow keys or a gamepad. In VR you aim with the controller.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,950
The big yellow ring snaps around the object you would hit. One button fires.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,950
The answer is decided the instant you press. The spear flight is only an animation.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,950
Each task has a rule: fastest spinner, slowest spinner, fastest swimmer, same as a picture, ...

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,450
Correct catches earn items from the reward table; the inventory is shown on the left.

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,950
All the art (sky, water, fish, rocks, trident) is a swappable 'skin' - more on that soon.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,950
Everything is set up on one node: RiverGame, inside main.tscn.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,150
Session: which experiment runs. It is a list of tasks (trials).

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,350
Skin: the visual look (scenery, models, spear). Clear it to get the plain version back.

11
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:05,050
Hide With Scenery: template nodes (floor, table) hidden while the skin's scenery is shown.

12
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:11,250
Input is picked automatically: VR controller with a headset, otherwise mouse, keyboard or gamepad.

13
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,750
To use another device, write a small AimInput script and drop it into Input Override.

14
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:22,250
The same session with the skin cleared: plain, high-contrast placeholder shapes.

15
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:28,750
Rules, timing and logs stay identical - the look never changes the experiment.

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,450
Each floating object is a RiverObjectConfig resource (a .tres file in river/data/objects).

17
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,950
Id: the name rules, rewards and logs use.

18
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,150
Movement Speed: metres per second along the river. 0 = stationary.

19
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,850
Rotation Speed: degrees per second around Rotation Axis.

20
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:56,550
Change a value in the Inspector, press Ctrl+S, and the next run uses it.

21
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:02,750
Scene: drag any .glb / .gltf / .tscn model here to replace the placeholder shape.

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,950
Models are scaled to Size (metres) and centred automatically, whatever their original scale.

23
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,150
Decoration lives in a RiverSkin: object id -> model, plus the scenery and the spear.

24
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:23,150
The game logic never reads the skin, so looks can be swapped without touching the experiment.

25
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:29,250
Wrap an imported model in a small scene with SkinModel: turn it to face downstream (+X) ...

26
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:32,250
... and it loops the model's swim animation.

27
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:39,950
The scenery scene holds water, sand, rocks, palms and the sky. Edit, replace or delete it freely.

28
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,950
Example experiment: find the character shown in a 2D picture among fast-spinning characters.

29
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,450
It lives in one folder: river/data/experiments/character_match.

30
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:58,650
One config per character. Here the model is the stimulus, so it goes in the config's Scene.

31
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,850
All five spin at 400 degrees per second ...

32
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:07,550
... and do not move, so rotation is the only thing that varies.

33
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:11,550
Tip: select several .tres files in the FileSystem dock to edit them all at once.

34
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:17,250
A SpawnPool lists the objects and how many to spawn per trial: here all 5.

35
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,950
A task is one trial. Text: the instruction on the panel.

36
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,150
Reference Image: the 2D picture to match.

37
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:30,850
Reference Object: which config is the correct answer.

38
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:38,050
Rule: StandardRule set to SAME_AS_REFERENCE. Other kinds: FASTEST/SLOWEST ROTATION or MOVEMENT, FROM_POOL.

39
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:43,250
Optional per trial: a fixed seed, retry until correct, and a time limit.

40
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:48,450
The session lists each of the 5 tasks twice: 10 trials.

41
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:54,650
Shuffle Tasks randomises the order. The seed is logged, so any run can be replayed exactly.

42
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:01,350
Make the 2D pictures with tools/reference_image_maker.tscn: set the folders ...

43
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,350
... and press F6. It renders a transparent PNG of every object config.

44
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:12,050
Finally, drag the session into RiverGame > Session and press F5.

45
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:18,050
Here it runs: the panel shows a 2D picture of one character.

46
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:24,050
Five characters spin at 400 degrees per second. The player spears the one in the picture.

47
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:30,050
Short feedback after each catch, then the next trial with a new random layout.

48
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:36,050
A wrong pick is logged too: which object, how long it took, and that it was wrong.

49
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:42,050
10 trials in shuffled order. The seed is saved, so the exact run can be replayed.

50
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:48,050
Change a speed, a picture or the number of trials in the .tres files - no code needed.

51
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:56,050
Need a new kind of question? Write a rule: extend TaskRule and override find_targets().

52
00:04:56,100 --> 00:05:00,550
This TagRule marks every object with a given tag as correct. It is about 15 lines.

53
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,550
New rules show up in the task's Rule menu, next to StandardRule.

54
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:12,550
Each session writes a CSV with one row per trial: target, selection, response time, correctness, misses.

55
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:17,550
Logs are in the user data folder; the exact path is printed when the game starts.

